In MongoDB, you can use a document-oriented model to store your data. Here's a simple way to structure your data:

```javascript
Catalog: {
 _id: ObjectId,
 name: String,
 metrics: [
 {
 _id: ObjectId,
 name: String,
 value: Number
 },
 // more metrics
 ]
}
```

In this model, each catalog is a document with an array of metrics. Each metric is an embedded document within the catalog document. This structure allows you to easily retrieve a catalog with all its metrics.

To retrieve this data using GraphQL, you would first define your types:

```graphql
type Metric {
 _id: ID!
 name: String!
 value: Float!
}

type Catalog {
 _id: ID!
 name: String!
 metrics: [Metric!]!
}
```

Then, you would define your resolvers. Here's a simple example of how you might define a resolver to get a catalog by its ID:

```javascript
const resolvers = {
 Query: {
 catalog: async (parent, args, context, info) => {
 return await context.db.collection('Catalog').findOne({ _id: args.id });
 },
 },
};
```

In this resolver, `context.db` is assumed to be an instance of the MongoDB client. The `findOne` method is used to retrieve a single document from the 'Catalog' collection that matches the provided ID.

Please note that this is a basic example and might need to be adjusted based on your specific use case and environment.

