Creating a Java program to solve linguistic puzzles requires a combination of natural language processing, a dictionary of words, and an algorithm to rearrange the given letters. One possible approach is using the WordNet dictionary and a permutation algorithm to find all possible combinations of the letters in the given word. You'll need to download and set up WordNet first (http://wordnet.princeton.edu/wordnet/download/current-version/).

Here's a basic outline for the code:

1. Import the necessary packages for WordNet and your Java program.
2. Create a dictionary from the WordNet data file.
3. Implement a permutation algorithm to generate all possible combinations of the given letters.
4. Check if each combination is a valid word in the dictionary.
5. Filter the valid words based on their synonyms or definitions to match the given meaning.

Here's a skeleton code to get you started:

```java
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.*;
import edu.smu.tspell.wordnet.*;

public class LinguisticPuzzleSolver {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 System.setProperty("wordnet.database.dir", "path/to/wordnet/dict");
 Synset[] synsets;
 NounSynset nounSynset;

 WordNetDatabase database = WordNetDatabase.getFileInstance();

 String[] puzzles = {"macin", "loipcat", "robed", "death", "detrsses"};
 String[] meanings = {
 "resistant to water",
 "to keep documents together",
 "ability to camouflage",
 "to remove unwanted hairs",
 "nervous or anxious",
 };

 for (int i = 0; i < puzzles.length; i++) {
 String solution = solvePuzzle(puzzles[i], meanings[i], database);
 System.out.println("Solution for puzzle (" + puzzles[i] + "): " + solution);
 }
 }

 public static String solvePuzzle(String puzzle, String meaning, WordNetDatabase database) {
 Set<String> combinations = generateCombinations(puzzle);
 for (String combination : combinations) {
 Synset[] synsets = database.getSynsets(combination);
 for (Synset synset : synsets) {
 String[] synonyms = synset.getWordForms();
 for (String synonym : synonyms) {
 if (synset.getDefinition().toLowerCase().contains(meaning)) {
 return combination;
 }
 }
 }
 }
 return null;
 }

 public static Set<String> generateCombinations(String s) {
 Set<String> result = new HashSet<>();
 if(s == null || s.length() == 0) {
 result.add("");
 return result;
 }
 char firstChar = s.charAt(0);
 String rem = s.substring(1);
 Set<String> words = generateCombinations(rem);
 for (String newString : words) {
 for (int i = 0; i <= newString.length(); i++) {
 result.add(newString.substring(0, i) + firstChar + newString.substring(i));
 }
 }
 return result;
 }
}
```

After setting up the WordNet directory and adjusting the path in the main method, you should be able to run it and see the solutions for the provided puzzles. This program offers a basic solution and won't account for all possible scenarios, but you can enhance it further as needed.

