In this code, a random quote from the JSON dataset is displayed each time the function displayQuote() is called. The quote and author are dynamically inserted into their respective HTML elements. How can I modify this code to display a new quote automatically every 10 seconds without the need for user interaction?

answer:
To modify the code to automatically display a new quote every 10 seconds, you can use the JavaScript function `setInterval()`. The setInterval function takes two arguments: the function you want to execute and the number of milliseconds between each execution.

Here's the modified code:

```javascript
const quotes = [
 {
 "quote": "Be the change you wish to see in the world.",
 "author": "Mahatma Gandhi"
 },
 {
 "quote": "Success is not final, failure is not fatal: It is the courage to continue that counts.",
 "author": "Winston Churchill"
 },
 {
 "quote": "Believe you can and you're halfway there.",
 "author": "Theodore Roosevelt"
 },
 {
 "quote": "The only way to do great work is to love what you do.",
 "author": "Steve Jobs"
 },
 {
 "quote": "Don't watch the clock; do what it does. Keep going.",
 "author": "Sam Levenson"
 }
];

function displayQuote() {
 const randomQuote = quotes[Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length)];
 const quoteElement = document.getElementById("quote");
 const authorElement = document.getElementById("author");
 quoteElement.innerText = randomQuote.quote;
 authorElement.innerText = "- " + randomQuote.author;
}

// Display a new quote immediately when the page loads
displayQuote();

// Display a new quote every 10000 milliseconds (10 seconds)
setInterval(displayQuote, 10000);
```

In this modified code, the `displayQuote()` function is called automatically when the page loads, thanks to the `displayQuote()` line outside of the function. Then, the `setInterval(displayQuote, 10000)` line sets up a timer that runs the `displayQuote()` function every 10 seconds (10000 milliseconds).

