1. Shader for Tank Treads:

You can use a simple scrolling UV shader to simulate the tank treads moving. Here's a basic example:

```csharp
Shader "Custom/ScrollingUVs" {
 Properties {
 _MainTex ("Texture", 2D) = "white" {}
 _ScrollSpeed ("Scroll Speed", Range(-10,10)) = 1
 }
 SubShader {
 Tags { "RenderType"="Opaque" }
 LOD 100

 Pass {
 CGPROGRAM
 #pragma vertex vert
 #pragma fragment frag

 #include "UnityCG.cginc"

 struct appdata {
 float4 vertex : POSITION;
 float2 uv : TEXCOORD0;
 };

 struct v2f {
 float2 uv : TEXCOORD0;
 float4 vertex : SV_POSITION;
 };

 sampler2D _MainTex;
 float _ScrollSpeed;

 v2f vert (appdata v) {
 v2f o;
 o.vertex = UnityObjectToClipPos(v.vertex);
 o.uv = v.uv;
 return o;
 }

 fixed4 frag (v2f i) : SV_Target {
 i.uv.x += _Time.y * _ScrollSpeed;
 fixed4 col = tex2D(_MainTex, i.uv);
 return col;
 }
 ENDCG
 }
 }
}
```
This shader will scroll the texture along the U (horizontal) axis. You can adjust the scroll speed in the material inspector.

2. Physics Simulation for Tank Treads:

Simulating tank treads with physics can be quite complex, especially if you want to simulate each link in the tread. Here's a simplified example where the tank tread is simulated as a single wheel:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class TankTreadSim : MonoBehaviour
{
 public float speed = 10f;
 public float torque = 10f;
 public Rigidbody rb;

 void Start()
 {
 rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody>();
 }

 void FixedUpdate()
 {
 float moveHorizontal = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
 float moveVertical = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

 Vector3 movement = transform.forward * moveVertical * speed;
 rb.AddForce(movement);

 float turn = moveHorizontal * torque;
 Quaternion turnRotation = Quaternion.Euler(0f, turn, 0f);
 rb.MoveRotation(rb.rotation * turnRotation);
 }
}
```
This script will move the tank forward and backward and apply torque for turning. You would attach this script to your tank tread object.

Please note that these are simplified examples and might not give you the exact result you want. You might need to adjust them according to your needs.

