Creating a list of potential titles for a feature film based on key themes, characters, and storyline using R code would require a complex algorithm that uses natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning. However, this is quite advanced and would require a large dataset to train the model.

Here's a simple example of how you might generate random titles using R. This example doesn't take into account the artistic vision, aesthetic of the film, or broad audience appeal, but it might give you a starting point:

```R
# Define the key themes, characters, and storyline
themes <- c("Love", "Betrayal", "Adventure", "Mystery")
characters <- c("John", "Sarah", "The Alien", "The Detective")
storyline <- c("In a Distant Galaxy", "In the Heart of the Jungle", "In the Depths of the Ocean", "In the Shadows of the City")

# Generate random titles
set.seed(123) # for reproducibility
generate_title <- function() {
 theme <- sample(themes, 1)
 character <- sample(characters, 1)
 story <- sample(storyline, 1)

 title <- paste(theme, "and", character, ":", story)

 # Check if the title is between 10-15 words
 if(length(strsplit(title, " ")[[1]]) >= 10 & length(strsplit(title, " ")[[1]]) <= 15) {
 return(title)
 } else {
 return(generate_title())
 }
}

# Generate a list of 10 potential titles
titles <- replicate(10, generate_title())
print(titles)
```

This code will generate 10 random titles based on the themes, characters, and storyline you've defined. Each title will be between 10-15 words long. Note that this is a very basic example and doesn't take into account the complexity of generating appealing and meaningful film titles.

