Here is a simple implementation of the Matrix Chain Multiplication problem using Dynamic Programming in C++. This program will find the optimal order to multiply the matrices and print the optimal cost.

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int MatrixChainOrder(int p[], int n) {
 int m[n][n];
 int i, j, k, L, q;
 for (i=1; i<n; i++)
 m[i][i] = 0;
 for (L=2; L<n; L++) {
 for (i=1; i<n-L+1; i++) {
 j = i+L-1;
 m[i][j] = INT_MAX;
 for (k=i; k<=j-1; k++) {
 q = m[i][k] + m[k+1][j] + p[i-1]*p[k]*p[j];
 if (q < m[i][j])
 m[i][j] = q;
 }
 }
 }
 return m[1][n-1];
}

int main() {
 int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
 int size = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
 cout<<"Minimum number of multiplications is "<<MatrixChainOrder(arr, size);
 return 0;
}
```

This program will print the minimum number of multiplications needed to multiply the chain of matrices. However, it does not print the optimal order of multiplication. The optimal order can be printed by modifying the above program to store the 'k' at which we split the product in a separate 2D array and then recursively following the splits.

Please note that the above code assumes that the array 'arr' represents the dimensions of the matrices such that the dimensions of the i-th matrix are arr[i-1] x arr[i].

Creating a highly intricate legal system for a fantasy world in C++ is a complex task that involves multiple aspects such as defining the laws, implementing the rules for different groups, and handling the repercussions for violations. Here's a simplified example of how you might start to structure this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <map>

// Define a Law class
class Law {
public:
 std::string name;
 std::string description;
 std::map<std::string, std::string> repercussions;

 Law(std::string name, std::string description) : name(name), description(description) {}
};

// Define a Group class
class Group {
public:
 std::string name;
 std::map<std::string, Law> laws;

 Group(std::string name) : name(name) {}

 void addLaw(Law law) {
 laws[law.name] = law;
 }
};

// Define a World class
class World {
public:
 std::map<std::string, Group> groups;

 void addGroup(Group group) {
 groups[group.name] = group;
 }

 void enforceLaw(std::string groupName, std::string lawName) {
 if (groups.find(groupName) != groups.end()) {
 if (groups[groupName].laws.find(lawName) != groups[groupName].laws.end()) {
 std::cout << "Enforcing law: " << lawName << " on group: " << groupName << std::endl;
 } else {
 std::cout << "No such law: " << lawName << " in group: " << groupName << std::endl;
 }
 } else {
 std::cout << "No such group: " << groupName << std::endl;
 }
 }
};

int main() {
 // Create a world
 World world;

 // Create a group
 Group elves("Elves");

 // Create a law
 Law law1("Law1", "Don't steal");
 law1.repercussions["first_time"] = "Warning";
 law1.repercussions["second_time"] = "Banishment";

 // Add the law to the group
 elves.addLaw(law1);

 // Add the group to the world
 world.addGroup(elves);

 // Enforce a law
 world.enforceLaw("Elves", "Law1");

 return 0;
}
```

This is a very basic example and doesn't cover all the intricacies you might need for a full-fledged legal system, but it should give you a starting point. You might want to consider using a database to store and retrieve your laws, groups, and repercussions, especially if you have a large number of each. You could also consider adding more functionality to handle different types of repercussions, track violations for each individual within a group, etc.

