 private void NewGame_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
 {
 _game.StartNewGame();
 }

 private void LoadGame_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
 {
 _game.LoadGame();
 }
}
```

This is a very basic structure. You'll need to expand it according to your game's requirements. For example, you might want to add more states, or create separate classes for each state to follow the Single Responsibility Principle (SOLID). You might also want to use the Command pattern to handle user input, or the Observer pattern to update the UI when the game state changes.

Remember to keep your code as simple as possible (KISS), don't add functionality until you need it (YAGNI), and don't repeat yourself (DRY). These principles will help you maintain a clean and manageable codebase.

