Sure, here's a simple Swift code snippet that can be used to identify the top 3 most commonly used verbs in the given text. This code uses the Natural Language Processing (NLP) capabilities of the Swift's NaturalLanguage framework. 

Please note that this code assumes that you have a macOS environment with Swift 5.1 or later.

```swift
import NaturalLanguage

let article = "The CRISPR-Cas9 innovation has facilitated the exact modification of genetic material, permitting scientists to alter DNA with unmatched precision and efficiency. This technology has a wide range of possible applications, including curing diseases, enhancing agriculture, and more."

let tagger = NLTagger(tagSchemes: [.lexicalClass])
tagger.string = article

let options: NLTagger.Options = [.omitPunctuation, .omitWhitespace, .joinNames]
let tags: [NLTag] = [.verb]

var verbCounts: [String: Int] = [:]

tagger.enumerateTags(in: article.startIndex..<article.endIndex, unit: .word, scheme: .lexicalClass, options: options) { tag, tokenRange in
 if let tag = tag, tags.contains(tag) {
 let verb = String(article[tokenRange]).lowercased()
 verbCounts[verb, default: 0] += 1
 }
 return true
}

let sortedVerbs = verbCounts.sorted { $0.value > $1.value }
let topThreeVerbs = Array(sortedVerbs.prefix(3))

for (verb, count) in topThreeVerbs {
 print("\(verb): \(count)")
}
```

This code will print out the top 3 most commonly used verbs in the text. Please note that this code does not handle verb conjugations, so different forms of the same verb will be counted separately.

