You can solve this problem by implementing a technique called "mouse locking" or "pointer locking". This technique confines the mouse pointer to the content area of the element and any movement of the mouse will not move the pointer. Instead, it will trigger an event and you can use this event to rotate the camera in your game.

Here's a basic example of how you can implement this in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Request pointer lock
var element = document.body; // Make the body the locked element
element.requestPointerLock = element.requestPointerLock || element.mozRequestPointerLock || element.webkitRequestPointerLock;
element.requestPointerLock();

// Listen to mouse movements
document.addEventListener("mousemove", function(e) {
 var movementX = e.movementX || e.mozMovementX || e.webkitMovementX || 0;
 var movementY = e.movementY || e.mozMovementY || e.webkitMovementY || 0;

 // Update the camera's direction based on mouse movement
 camera.rotation.y -= movementX * sensitivity;
 camera.rotation.x -= movementY * sensitivity;
}, false);
```

In this example, `sensitivity` is a variable that you can adjust to control how much the camera rotates for a given mouse movement. You'll also need to add some code to handle exiting pointer lock mode, and you may want to only enter pointer lock mode in response to a user action, like clicking on the canvas.

Remember to check browser compatibility for pointer lock API and provide fallbacks or instructions for unsupported browsers.

Three.ArcballControls is not a native control in Three.js; instead, you need to use an external library called `ArcballControls`. However, you can achieve a 360-degree camera rotation with the native `OrbitControls` in Three.js.

In the following example, I'll show you how to set up a simple scene with a cube and `OrbitControls` for rotating the camera around the cube:

1. First, load required scripts:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
 <meta charset="utf-8">
</head>
<body>
 <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/three.js/r128/three.min.js"></script>
 <script src="https://threejs.org/examples/jsm/controls/OrbitControls.js"></script>
 <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a new `script.js` file and implement the following code:

```javascript
// Set up the scene, camera, and renderer
let scene = new THREE.Scene();
let camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
let renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({antialias: true});
renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

// Set up OrbitControls
const controls = new THREE.OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);

// Create a cube
let geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry();
let material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color: 0x00ff00});
let cube = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
scene.add(cube);

// Set camera position
camera.position.z = 5;
camera.position.y = 1;
controls.update();

function animate() {
 requestAnimationFrame(animate);

 // Render the scene
 controls.update();
 renderer.render(scene, camera);
}

animate();
```

Now you can use your mouse or touchpad to rotate the camera 360 degrees around the cube. The `OrbitControls` allow you to orbit the camera around the target (which is the center by default) by dragging the mouse/touchpad.

By adjusting the `camera.position` and the `controls.target` properties, you can change the initial position of the camera and the object it orbits.

Sure, here's a basic example of a URL shortening script in PHP. This script uses a simple base62 encoding for the URL shortening algorithm. It also uses MySQL for the database. 

```php
<?php
$host = "localhost";
$db = "url_shortener";
$user = "root";
$pass = "";

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($host, $user, $pass, $db);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
 die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

function generateShortCode($id) {
 $characters = '0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
 $shortCode = '';
 while ($id > 0) {
 $shortCode = $characters[$id % 62] . $shortCode;
 $id = floor($id / 62);
 }
 return $shortCode;
}

function shortenURL($url) {
 global $conn;
 $query = "INSERT INTO urls (url) VALUES (?)";
 $stmt = $conn->prepare($query);
 $stmt->bind_param("s", $url);
 $stmt->execute();
 $id = $stmt->insert_id;
 $shortCode = generateShortCode($id);
 $query = "UPDATE urls SET short_code = ? WHERE id = ?";
 $stmt = $conn->prepare($query);
 $stmt->bind_param("si", $shortCode, $id);
 $stmt->execute();
 return $shortCode;
}

$url = "https://www.example.com";
$shortCode = shortenURL($url);
echo "Shortened URL: http://yourdomain.com/$shortCode\n";
?>
```

This script assumes that you have a table named `urls` in your database with the following structure:

```sql
CREATE TABLE urls (
 id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
 url VARCHAR(2048) NOT NULL,
 short_code VARCHAR(10)
);
```

Please replace "localhost", "root", "", and "url_shortener" with your actual database host, username, password, and database name. Also, replace "http://yourdomain.com/" with your actual domain. 

This is a very basic example and doesn't include any error checking or security measures. You should add those in a production environment.

