Creating a custom WordPress block involves using JavaScript and PHP. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a custom WordPress block with a heading, paragraph, and image using Gutenberg, the block editor in WordPress.

1. **Set Up Your Development Environment**

 First, you need to set up a local development environment. You can use tools like Local by Flywheel, MAMP, or XAMPP to create a local WordPress site.

2. **Install Node.js and NPM**

 Gutenberg is built using JavaScript, so you'll need Node.js and NPM (Node Package Manager) installed on your computer. You can download Node.js from the official website and NPM is included in the installation.

3. **Install the Create Guten Block Toolkit**

 Create Guten Block is a toolkit that simplifies the process of creating custom Gutenberg blocks. You can install it globally on your computer by running the following command in your terminal:

 ```
 npm install --global create-guten-block
 ```

4. **Create a New Block**

 Navigate to your WordPress plugins directory and run the following command to create a new block:

 ```
 npx create-guten-block my-block
 ```

 Replace "my-block" with the name of your block.

5. **Edit Your Block**

 The above command will create a new directory with the name of your block. Inside this directory, you'll find a `src/block/block.js` file. This is where you'll define your block.

 Here's a simple example of a block with a heading, paragraph, and image:

 ```javascript
 const { registerBlockType } = wp.blocks;
 const { RichText, MediaUpload, BlockControls } = wp.editor;
 const { Button } = wp.components;

 registerBlockType('my-block/my-block', {
 title: 'My Block',
 icon: 'smiley',
 category: 'common',
 attributes: {
 title: {
 type: 'string',
 source: 'html',
 selector: 'h2',
 },
 body: {
 type: 'string',
 source: 'html',
 selector: 'p',
 },
 imageAlt: {
 attribute: 'alt',
 selector: '.my-block-image',
 },
 imageUrl: {
 attribute: 'src',
 selector: '.my-block-image',
 },
 },
 edit({ attributes, className, setAttributes }) {
 const getImageButton = (openEvent) => {
 if(attributes.imageUrl) {
 return (
 <img 
 src={ attributes.imageUrl }
 onClick={ openEvent }
 className="image"
 />
 );
 }
 else {
 return (
 <div className="button-container">
 <Button 
 onClick={ openEvent }
 className="button button-large"
 >
 Pick an image
 </Button>
 </div>
 );
 }
 };

 return (
 <div className={ className }>
 <RichText
 tagName="h2"
 placeholder="Your heading"
 value={ attributes.title }
 onChange={ (value) => setAttributes({ title: value }) }
 />
 <RichText
 tagName="p"
 placeholder="Your paragraph"
 value={ attributes.body }
 onChange={ (value) => setAttributes({ body: value }) }
 />
 <BlockControls>
 <MediaUpload
 onSelect={ (media) => { setAttributes({ imageAlt: media.alt, imageUrl: media.url }); } }
 type="image"
 value={ attributes.imageID }
 render={ ({ open }) => getImageButton(open) }
 />
 </BlockControls>
 </div>
 );
 },
 save({ attributes }) {
 return (
 <div>
 <h2>{ attributes.title }</h2>
 <p>{ attributes.body }</p>
 <img 
 src={ attributes.imageUrl }
 alt={ attributes.imageAlt }
 />
 </div>
 );
 },
 });
 ```

6. **Build and Enqueue Your Block**

 After you've defined your block, you need to build it and enqueue it in WordPress. Run the following command in your block's directory to build it:

 ```
 npm run build
 ```

 Then, open the `plugin.php` file in your block's directory and enqueue your block's `build/index.js` and `build/style.css` files:

 ```php
 function my_block_enqueue() {
 wp_enqueue_script(
 'my-block',
 plugins_url('build/index.js', __FILE__),
 array('wp-blocks', 'wp-i18n', 'wp-element', 'wp-editor'),
 filemtime(plugin_dir_path(__FILE__) . 'build/index.js')
 );

 wp_enqueue_style(
 'my-block',
 plugins_url('build/style.css', __FILE__),
 array(),
 filemtime(plugin_dir_path(__FILE__) . 'build/style.css')
 );
 }
 add_action('enqueue_block_editor_assets', 'my_block_enqueue');
 ```

7. **Activate Your Block**

 Finally, log in to your WordPress admin area, go to the Plugins page, and activate your block.

Now, you should be able to add your custom block to any post or page using the block editor.

To create a React component in a Next.js application with the features you've described, you'll need to use multiple libraries and have a server-side function to handle the file uploading and parsing. Here's a high-level overview of how you can create such a component:

1. Install necessary libraries for the project:
 - `npm install react-dropzone`
 - `npm install azure-storage`
 - `npm install fcs-parser`
 - `npm install plotly.js`

2. Create a new React component called `FileDropzone.js`:

```javascript
import React, { useCallback, useState } from 'react';
import { useDropzone } from 'react-dropzone';
import axios from 'axios';
import Plot from 'react-plotly.js';

const FileDropzone = () => {
 const [uploadedFiles, setUploadedFiles] = useState([]);

 const onDrop = useCallback(async (acceptedFiles) => {
 const promises = acceptedFiles.map((file) => {
 const formData = new FormData();
 formData.append('file', file);
 return axios.post('/api/upload', formData);
 });

 const results = await Promise.all(promises);
 setUploadedFiles(results.map((res) => res.data));
 }, []);

 const { getRootProps, getInputProps, isDragActive } = useDropzone({ onDrop });

 return (
 <div {...getRootProps()}>
 <input {...getInputProps()} />
 {isDragActive ? <p>Drop the files here ...</p> : <p>Drag and drop some files here, or click to select files</p>}
 <div>
 {uploadedFiles.map((file) => (
 <div key={file.fileName} style={{ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: 'repeat(12, 1fr)', gridGap: '10px' }}>
 <h3>{file.fileName}</h3>
 <Plot data={file.plotData} layout={file.plotLayout} />
 <pre>{JSON.stringify(file.parsedHeaders, null, 2)}</pre>
 </div>
 ))}
 </div>
 </div>
 );
};

export default FileDropzone;
```

3. Create a server-side API route `/api/upload.js` to handle file uploads, parsing, and response:

```javascript
import { parseString } from 'fcs-parser';
import { BlobServiceClient } from '@azure/storage-blob';

export default async (req, res) => {
 const AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING = process.env.AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING;
 const blobServiceClient = BlobServiceClient.fromConnectionString(AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING);
 const containerClient = blobServiceClient.getContainerClient('fcs-files');

 if (!containerClient.exists()) {
 await containerClient.create();
 }

 const file = req.file;
 const blockBlobClient = containerClient.getBlockBlobClient(file.originalname);
 await blockBlobClient.upload(file.buffer, file.size);

 const parsedFcs = parseString(file.buffer.toString());
 const plotData = {/* Generate plot data using FCS data and Plotly.js */};
 const plotLayout = {/* Generate plot layout using Plotly.js */};

 res.json({
 fileName: file.originalname,
 parsedHeaders: parsedFcs.header,
 plotData,
 plotLayout,
 });
};
```

4. Add the necessary configuration in your `/pages/api/_middleware.js` to handle file uploads:

```javascript
import nextConnect from 'next-connect';
import multer from 'multer';

const upload = multer();

export default nextConnect({
 onError(error, req, res) {
 console.error('error', error);
 res.status(501).json({ error: `Sorry, something happened! ${error.message}` });
 },
 onNoMatch(req, res) {
 res.status(405).json({ error: `Method '${req.method}' not allowed.` });
 },
}).use(upload.single('file'));
```

5. Don't forget to include the `FileDropzone` component in your desired page within the Next.js application.

This example implementation assumes that you have properly set up the Azure Blob Storage and provided the necessary credentials. Also, remember to adjust the Plotly.js code to generate the appropriate data and layout. Ideally, you would create a separate function for generating plot-related information.

