In TypeScript, you can use the `keyof` and `Extract` utility types to create a type that includes only the properties that are in both A and B. Here's how you can define such a type:

```typescript
type A = {
 prop1: string;
 prop2: number;
 // other properties...
};

type B = {
 prop2: number;
 prop3: boolean;
 // other properties...
};

type Both = {
 [K in Extract<keyof A, keyof B>]: A[K];
};
```

In this example, the `Both` type will only include the `prop2` property, which is present in both `A` and `B`. The `Extract` utility type is used to create a union of the keys that are present in both `A` and `B`, and then this union is used as the keys of the `Both` type. The type of each property in `Both` is the same as the type of the corresponding property in `A`.

